\n\n#### 1. Operational Assessment & Decision Guidance\n- **Verdict:** ⚠️ Elevated thermal stress risk for St. Mary's coral reef and Netravati‑Gurupuru mangroves due to intersecting hazard footprint and nearby anthropogenic pressures.\n- **Metrics:** Hazard zone includes 1 critical ecological habitat (Netravati‑Gurupura Estuarine Mangroves) and 764,707 residents; live ocean observation at 12.85°N, 74.84°E shows significant wave height 0.68 m (swell 0.48 m, period 9.0 s).\n- **Action 1:** Deploy continuous sea‑surface temperature and heat‑stress loggers at St. Mary's reef and mangrove sites; integrate data with existing wave telemetry for real‑time stress indices.\n- **Action 2:** Coordinate with New Mangalore Port, Old Mangalore Port, and Malpe Fishing Harbour to implement runoff cooling measures (e.g., shaded discharge zones, temperature‑controlled effluent) and restrict high‑heat activities during peak thermal periods.\n\n#### 2. Specialist Observations & Findings\n- **[Finding #1]** Estimated 764,707 residents across 7 coastal settlement(s) (Mangaluru City Corporation, Ullal Coastal Municipality, Surathkal Coastal Settlement, Udupi City Municipality and 3 others) fall within the hazard footprint. `764,707 people` *(Status: VERIFIED)*\n- **[Finding #2]** 3 maritime port/harbour facility(ies) intersect the hazard footprint: New Mangalore Port (NMPT), Old Mangalore Port (Bunder), Malpe Fishing Harbour. `3 ports` *(Status: VERIFIED)*\n- **[Finding #3]** 2 apex healthcare facility(ies) located within the hazard zone: District Wenlock Hospital, Mangaluru, District Government Hospital, Udupi. `2 facilities` *(Status: VERIFIED)*\n- **[Finding #4]** 1 critical ecological habitat(s) intersect the hazard zone: Netravati-Gurupura Estuarine Mangroves. `1 habitats` *(Status: VERIFIED)*\n- **[Finding #5]** Live ocean observation at (12.85°N, 74.84°E) records significant wave height of 0.68 m (swell height 0.48 m, wave period 9.0 s, wind wave 0.00 m). *(Status: VERIFIED)*\n\n###